Sure. The important thing is **MAVLink is the communication protocol**. 
Python and C++ are just two different ways of using it.

### Think of it like this

```text
ArduPilot
    │
    │ MAVLink messages
    │
    ├──────────────► Python program
    │
    └──────────────► C++ program
```

Both can receive the same information:

```text
GPS position
Altitude
Speed
Battery
Flight mode
Armed/disarmed
```

And both can send commands:

```text
ARM
DISARM
TAKEOFF
LAND
CHANGE MODE
GO TO WAYPOINT
```

### So what's the difference?

Python has a library called **pymavlink** that makes this relatively straightforward:

```text
Python
   ↓
pymavlink
   ↓
MAVLink
   ↓
ArduPilot
```

With C++, you can also use MAVLink, but you generally have to deal with more of the communication details yourself:

```text
C++
 ↓
MAVLink C/C++ headers/library
 ↓
Message creation/parsing
 ↓
MAVLink
 ↓
ArduPilot
```

So when I said **"C++ is more hands-on"**, I meant:

> **Python gives you more ready-to-use convenience; C++ gives you more control but you may write more code.**

### For your situation

Since you are interested in **C++ console applications**, you could completely skip Python:

```text
             ArduPilot SITL
                   ↑
                   │ MAVLink
                   ↓
          ┌─────────────────┐
          │   Your C++ App  │
          ├─────────────────┤
          │ Telemetry       │
          │ Mission         │
          │ Vehicle Status  │
          │ Commands        │
          └─────────────────┘
                   │
              Console UI
```

You don't need Python just because you're using Mission Planner.

**Mission Planner + ArduPilot SITL + your C++ program** is a perfectly valid setup.

If your goal is to learn **C++ + UAV/GCS concepts**, 
I would actually recommend starting with a **small C++ telemetry monitor** rather than Python.
